  emoji: '🏅',
  chapterText:
      'Nikhil had always finished last in every race at his school in Anand, and by Class 5 he had almost '
      'stopped trying, convinced that he simply lacked the speed other boys seemed to have naturally. His '
      'physical education teacher, Govind sir, noticed that Nikhil ran with his shoulders hunched and his '
      'breathing uneven, wasting energy before he had even crossed halfway. Instead of scolding him for '
      'finishing behind everyone else, Govind sir asked Nikhil to stay back one afternoon and quietly observed '
      'how he moved.\n\n'
      'He explained that Nikhil\'s posture was working against him, and that running well required a steady '
      'rhythm rather than a sudden burst of effort followed by exhaustion. Over the following weeks, Govind '
      'sir taught him to breathe evenly, to swing his arms in rhythm with his legs, and to pace himself '
      'instead of sprinting wildly at the start. Nikhil practised every morning before school, often alone on '
      'the empty ground, ignoring the mild ridicule of classmates who joked that improvement was impossible for '
      'him.\n\n'
      'Slowly, his stamina improved, and he no longer felt breathless after the first lap. At the annual sports '
      'day, Nikhil lined up nervously for the four-hundred-metre race, half expecting the same humiliating '
      'result as every year before. This time, however, he paced himself carefully, remembering his teacher\'s '
      'advice, and reserved his energy for the final stretch. As his classmates gasped, Nikhil surged forward '
